summary:
- topic: Elementary Reconfiguration Update
  text: Dr. Jo Prince presented survey data from recent community forums on the five
    proposed elementary school reconfiguration maps. The data showed strong support
    for Map 1 (current configuration) and Map 2, with significant opposition to Map
    4 (grade-banding) and Map 5 (socioeconomic balancing). The steering committee
    will review this feedback along with upcoming staff input to bring two proposals
    to the board for a workshop on October 26th.
- topic: Bullying Prevention and Response
  text: SPMS Principal Dr. Rebecca Stern detailed the district's K-12 tiered approach
    to preventing and responding to bullying, emphasizing the legal definition and
    the focus on creating a culture of belonging. Data showed very few substantiated
    bullying cases reported to the state, prompting board questions about the high
    bar for substantiation versus the reality of student misbehavior, which is still
    addressed through restorative practices.
- topic: Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Education
  text: Director of Technology Andy Wallace updated the board on the AI Advisory Committee's
    work, which aims to develop guidelines, policies, and educational materials by
    January. A lengthy board discussion followed on a proposed moratorium on student
    AI use, which was amended to apply to grades K-8 but was ultimately withdrawn
    to allow for more community and staff engagement before enacting policy.
- topic: Public Comment on Staffing and Negotiations
  text: SPESPA President Connie DeSanto addressed the board regarding what she described
    as dangerously low staffing levels for Ed Techs, attributing the issue to budget
    cuts. She stated that the union has been open to temporary solutions like subcontracting
    but has been frustrated with negotiations and has sought a mediator, pushing back
    on claims that the union is preventing the district from staffing classrooms.
- topic: Public Participation Policy (BEDH)
  text: The board held a second reading and voted to approve changes to the public
    participation policy. The key change eliminates the ability for speakers to yield
    their time to another person. The policy was passed on a 4-2 vote after public
    comment and board discussion, with some members supporting it as a necessary step
    and others arguing it unnecessarily limits public voice.
- topic: Facilities and Capital Planning
  text: The Buildings & Grounds Committee reported that the SPHS Athletic Complex
    project remains on track and on budget, with construction to begin in spring 2027.
    The committee also announced plans to engage a consultant for a long-term district-wide
    facilities assessment and discussed the future use of the Kaler Elementary building,
    which reverts to the city in five years if not used by the district.
